title: 'Temporal Equivalence Principle: A Proper-Time Domain Boundary in the Outer Solar System'
abstract: |
  The orbital alignment of extreme trans-Neptunian objects (TNOs) is conventionally attributed to a distant, undiscovered point mass. This paper tests a deterministic alternative under the Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP), in which the anomaly is modelled as a spatially fixed proper-time field gradient — a localized domain boundary — rather than a shepherding planet. Two dynamically distinct populations, drawn from the JPL Small-Body Database and the independent CODE cometary catalogue, isolate the same 60-degree sector of inertial sky. Detached extreme TNOs cluster about a common perihelion direction (R_varpi = 0.332, p = 0.00735 under a measured discovery-footprint null calibrated on the characterized OSSOS ensemble), and long-period comets arriving from that sector carry a significant orbit-reconstruction discrepancy: an in-plane orbital rotation localized to the perihelion element and weighted to the inbound trajectory (inbound p = 0.042; outbound p = 0.547), occurring without orbital energy exchange and persisting after regression of a REBOUND N-body planetary baseline, of observational leverage, and of each orbit's own published error budget. The comets' aphelion directions — a spatial observable no orbit reconstruction can manufacture — dipole toward the same axis at the 1e-2 to 1e-4 level under tide-aware nulls. An independent Levenberg-Marquardt two-leg refit of the raw Minor Planet Center astrometry on 586 pre-2018 comets, a record built without any Warsaw-lineage input, reproduces the per-comet boundary rotation at rho = +0.998 and retains the inbound-leg in-cap excess (p = 0.0341; p = 0.00695 on the CODE-overlap seat). On the post-2017 prospective cohort the modern record's dominant structure replicates as a lapse-slip anomaly of the identical signature class at the opposite polarity of a bipolar axis anchored near the CMB rest frame (joint opposite-polarity test p = 0.000100), while the reconstruction-free aphelion channel independently replicates the declared-axis lean quality-flat across every measured stratum (d_par = +0.0949, p = 0.00215 under the tide-aware null). A ten-channel global synthesis spanning six catalogue lineages (SBDB, DES, MPCORB, CODE, Warsaw, CometEls) yields an omnibus statistic S_10 = 186.1 (p = 0.0118 against a 20,000-draw random-axis permutation null). These multi-messenger, multi-population data provide mutually reinforcing evidence for a non-integrable dynamical time field in the outer solar system.
